"[1102.5433] On the van der Waerden numbers w(2;3,t) (https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.1102.5433)\nciteturn6search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 15.7 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; View a PDF of the paper titled On the van der Waerden numbers w(2;3,t), by Tanbir Ahmed and Oliver Kullmann and Hunter Snevily ... We have computed the new number w(2;3,19) = 349, and we provide lower bounds for 20 <= t <= 39, where for t <= 30 we conjecture these lower bounds to be exact.\n\n# Mathematics > Combinatorics\n\n[Submitted on 26 Feb 2011 (v1), last revised 5 Mar 2014 (this version, v4)]\n\n# Title:On the van der Waerden numbers w(2;3,t)\n\nAuthors:Tanbir Ahmed, Oliver Kullmann, Hunter Snevily\n\nView a PDF of the paper titled On the van der Waerden numbers w(2;3,t), by Tanbir Ahmed and Oliver Kullmann and Hunter Snevily\n\nView PDF\n\n> Abstract:We present results and conjectures on the van der Waerden numbers w(2;3,t) and on the new palindromic van der Waerden numbers pdw(2;3,t). We have computed the new number w(2;3,19) = 349, and we provide lower bounds for 20 <= t <= 39, where for t <= 30 we conjecture these lower bounds to be exact. The lower bounds for 24 <= t <= 30 refute the conjecture that w(2;3,t) <= t^2, and we present an improved conjecture. We also investigate regularities in the good partitions (certificates) to better understand the lower bounds.\n> Motivated by such reglarities, we introduce *palindromic van der Waerden numbers* pdw(k; t_0,...,t_{k-1}), defined as ordinary van der Waerden numbers w(k; t_0,...,t_{k-1}), however only allowing palindromic solutions (good partitions), defined as reading the same from both ends. Different from the situation for ordinary van der Waerden numbers, these \"numbers\" need actually to be pairs of numbers. We compute pdw(2;3,t) for 3 <= t <= 27, and we provide lower bounds, which we conjecture to be exact, for t <= 35.\n> All computations are based on SAT solving, and we discuss the various relations between SAT solving and Ramsey theory. Especially we introduce a novel (open-source) SAT solver, the tawSolver, which performs best on the SAT instances studied here, and which is actually the original DLL-solver, but with an efficient implementation and a modern heuristic typical for look-ahead solvers (applying the theory developed in the SAT handbook article of the second author).\n\nComments:  | Second version 25 pages, updates of numerical data, improved formulations, and extended discussions on SAT. Third version 42 pages, with SAT solver data (especially for new SAT solver) and improved representation. Fourth version 47 pages, with updates and added explanations\nSubjects:  | Combinatorics (math.CO); Data Structures and Algorithms (cs.DS)\nMSC (Mathematical Subject Classification) classes:  | 05D10\nACM (Association of Computing Machinery Classification) classes:  | F.2.2; G.2.2; I.2.8\nCite as:  | arXiv:1102.5433 [math.CO]\n   | https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.1102.5433 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nJournal reference:  | Discrete Applied Mathematics 174: 27-51 (2014)\nRelated DOI (Digital Object Identifier):  | https://doi.org/10.1016/j.dam.2014.05.007 DOI(s) linking to related resources\n\n## Submission history\n\nFrom: Oliver Kullmann [view email]\n[v1] Sat, 26 Feb 2011 18:30:11 UTC (34 KB)\n[v2] Thu, 27 Oct 2011 16:44:59 UTC (50 KB)\n[v3] Thu, 29 Aug 2013 21:23:19 UTC (56 KB)\n[v4] Wed, 5 Mar 2014 23:53:26 UTC (61 KB)\n\nFull-text links:\n\n## Access Paper:\n\nView a PDF of the paper titled On the van der Waerden numbers w(2;3,t), by Tanbir Ahmed and Oliver Kullmann and Hunter Snevily\n\n  * View PDF\n  * TeX Source\n\nview license\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn the van der Waerden numbers w(2;3,t) (https://arxiv.org/abs/1102.5433)\nciteturn6academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 15.7 years ago; We have computed the new number w(2;3,19) = 349, and we provide lower bounds for 20 <= t <= 39, where for t <= 30 we conjecture these lower bounds to be exact.\nTitle: On the van der Waerden numbers w(2;3,t)\nAuthors: Tanbir Ahmed, Oliver Kullmann, Hunter Snevily\nDate: Sat Feb 26 18:30:11 2011\n\nWe present results and conjectures on the van der Waerden numbers w(2;3,t) and on the new palindromic van der Waerden numbers pdw(2;3,t). We have computed the new number w(2;3,19) = 349, and we provide lower bounds for 20 <= t <= 39, where for t <= 30 we conjecture these lower bounds to be exact. The lower bounds for 24 <= t <= 30 refute the conjecture that w(2;3,t) <= t^2, and we present an improved conjecture. We also investigate regularities in the good partitions (certificates) to better understand the lower bounds. Motivated by such reglarities, we introduce *palindromic van der Waerden numbers* pdw(k; t_0,...,t_{k-1}), defined as ordinary van der Waerden numbers w(k; t_0,...,t_{k-1}), however only allowing palindromic solutions (good partitions), defined as reading the same from both ends. Different from the situation for ordinary van der Waerden numbers, these \"numbers\" need actually to be pairs of numbers. We compute pdw(2;3,t) for 3 <= t <= 27, and we provide lower bounds, which we conjecture to be exact, for t <= 35. All computations are based on SAT solving, and we discuss the various relations between SAT solving and Ramsey theory. Especially we introduce a novel (open-source) SAT solver, the tawSolver, which performs best on the SAT instances studied here, and which is actually the original DLL-solver, but with an efficient implementation and a modern heuristic typical for look-ahead solvers (applying the theory developed in the SAT handbook article of the second author).--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Latin Tableau Conjecture (https://www.combinatorics.org/ojs/index.php/eljc/article/download/v32i2p48/pdf/)\nciteturn6search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.4 years ago; Submitted: Jul 31, 2024; Accepted: Apr 5, 2025; Published: J